WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.980
By watching this video, you've taken advantage of software created by an open source community of developers.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.020
But what does that really mean, "open source"?

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:10.349
Is it just another way of saying "free" software?

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:17.670
Well, it's true that open source software is distributed at no cost, but that's missing a much larger point.

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:19.579
It's really about freedom.

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:26.199
By this, I mean the freedom for users and developers do much more with open source software.

00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:28.629
Okay, a quick bit of history.

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:30.370
How did open source come about?

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:35.319
Years ago, most large software projects happened inside private companies.

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:39.280
They sell their proprietary software, typically with after-sales support.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:43.420
Then, in the early eighties, interest in open source projects picked up.

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:46.749
But it really took off about a decade later with Linux.

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:57.120
That's when developers began combining the two approaches, mixing proprietary software with open source.

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:02.369
Red Hat is one of the best known examples with Red Hat Enterprise Linux, or RHEL.

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:09.584
But RHEL has an open source counterpart called CentOS.

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:13.983
It's the free version of RHEL.

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:18.930
So why did Red Hat donate the CentOS source code to an open source community?

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:20.430
It's not about charity.

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:25.439
Like a lot of for-profit companies, they've worked out a win-win situation.

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:30.360
On one hand, the company gets help building and improving the base of their commercial product.

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:36.629
On the other, open source devs get commercial-grade code at no cost and the freedom to extend it.

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:43.015
Okay, with that history behind us, let's cover the mechanics and answer a few who-does-what questions.

00:01:43.015 --> 00:01:48.486
As I mentioned earlier, open source allows you to improve and build upon a base.

00:01:48.501 --> 00:01:51.780
To get that done, you first need a few things.

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:54.599
We begin with a repository.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:01.449
If you're going to share and collaborate, you need to make it easy to access.

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:10.749
Repositories, or repos as most developers call them, are collaboration platforms like GitHub where you can get source code, report bugs or suggest improvements.

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:16.479
But more importantly, developers can contribute code that improves or fixes the existing code base.

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:22.029
The repo is accessible and writeable anywhere, so anyone can propose changes.

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:25.720
The authors of these changes are referred to as contributors.

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:33.939
They create their own local copy of the official repo and make changes.

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:39.309
They ask for their change to be merged into the official code with what's called a pull request.

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:46.246
Anyone can be a contributor, but established members are what are called committers.

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:52.240
They have a review authority on any proposed changes.

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:55.006
A committer is responsible for evaluating the pull request.

00:02:55.006 --> 00:03:02.011
The pull command copies down their change, and then they have a chance to review it.

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:06.370
If they agree with the changes, it's merged back into the main code.

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:13.300
Committers enforce code standards and usually require test code to demonstrate the code works as promised.

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:19.960
Becoming a committer requires you have a proven track record and other committers who are willing to vouch for the quality of your work.

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:26.189
It's worth mentioning here that there's no requirement that an open source project be a certain size.

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:29.902
It can be a one-person passion project, or a huge developer effort.

00:03:29.902 --> 00:03:36.749
No matter its size, thanks to a common collaboration platform, open source isn't a free-for-all development project.

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:41.009
In fact, once a project reaches a significant number of interested parties, 

00:03:41.009 --> 00:03:47.180
and especially commercial interests, the handling of future changes and plans gets a lot more formal.

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:50.849
This formal designation typically follows a pretty standard naming convention.

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:54.264
It's the project name plus the word "foundation".

00:04:00.410 --> 00:04:04.969
You think of Apache Foundation, Linux Foundation, Eclipse Foundation -- you get the idea.

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:09.031
Okay, this brings us to a final player: lawyers.

00:04:09.031 --> 00:04:18.005
Or, more accurately, the open source license that spells out how the source could be distributed, modified and other stuff like attribution.

00:04:18.005 --> 00:04:20.230
The most common is the GPL.

00:04:21.670 --> 00:04:23.410
That's the General Public License.

00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:29.529
It guarantees four freedoms: the freedom to run, study, share and modify.

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:32.589
This includes commercial for-profit uses too.

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:38.130
Finally, it's a bit of a cliché, but success of an open source project takes a village.

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:44.100
The common term is a community.
It is made up of users, contributors, committers and foundation members.

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:49.020
So basically, an open source project starts with a great idea, developers, and some code.

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:52.170
But projects don't have to start from scratch.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:54.600
They might start another open source project.

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:58.889
One of the best known examples is Linux, which is based on Unix.

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:05.420
If an open source project can get community interest, it gains contributors.

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:07.222
Contributors eventually become committers.

00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:10.808
That user-contributor-committer cycle continues.

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:18.920
With a good idea and a bit of luck, a project gets enough regular contributors and committers that its growth becomes self-sustaining.

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:23.060
The cycle can also be turbocharged by industry contributions.

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:25.706
For example, Kubernetes was open sourced by Google.

00:05:25.776 --> 00:05:31.820
Since it was based on their internal development, it benefited from Google's experience with container orchestration.

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:37.709
Popularity of Kubernetes benefits adopters by allowing them rely on a foundational layer of software.

00:05:37.717 --> 00:05:44.629
Developers can then focus their efforts on other projects, just like Red Hat did with RHEL and OpenShift.

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:48.529
So what can you do if you want to join the open source movement?

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:52.490
Start by becoming a contributor -- or just reporting bugs.

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:56.629
Once you have experience with the code, start by contributing bug fixes.

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:01.069
Once a few of your submissions are accepted, you're well on your way to becoming a committer.

